 ## Subtopics
 
+### [PCA Pose Alignment](7626-pca-pose-alignment.md)
+PCA Pose Alignment recenters and rotates a set of camera poses so that the principal components of the camera position distribution align with the global XYZ axes. It solves the problem of arbitrary coordinate system orientation in multi-view datasets by enforcing a canonical frame derived from the data geometry. Unlike [Sim(3) Alignment via Procrustes Analysis](8526-sim-3-alignment-via-procrustes-analysis.md), which minimizes rigid-body transformation errors to match a target configuration, PCA Pose Alignment relies solely on the statistical variance of camera centers, ignoring orientation constraints during the rotation estimation.
+
 ### [Sim(3) Alignment via Procrustes Analysis](8526-sim-3-alignment-via-procrustes-analysis.md)
-A method to align estimated camera poses to ground truth by computing a 3D similarity transformation (Sim(3)) using Procrustes analysis on camera pose locations. Unlike Pose Recentering which only applies a rigid transformation (rotation and translation) to center the scene, this method also scales the scene to match the ground truth scale. It is chosen when absolute scale information is available and needs to be recovered, whereas Pose Recentering is used when only relative pose structure is available and scale is arbitrary.
-
-### [PCA Pose Alignment](7626-pca-pose-alignment.md)
-A variant that transforms poses so that the principal components of the camera positions lie on the XYZ axes. It recenters the camera positions by subtracting their mean and then computes the eigendecomposition of the covariance matrix of the centered positions. The eigenvectors are sorted by eigenvalue and assigned to the rotation matrix. If the determinant of the rotation matrix is negative, the third axis is flipped to ensure a proper rotation. The transform matrix is constructed by concatenating the rotation matrix and the rotated negative mean position. A final check flips the y and z axes if the mean z-component of the y-axis of the recentered poses is negative, ensuring a consistent orientation. This method aligns the coordinate system with the data's principal axes rather than the average camera orientation, which can be beneficial for scenes with specific geometric structures.
+Sim(3) Alignment via Procrustes Analysis computes a 3D similarity transformation (rotation, translation, and uniform scale) to align estimated camera poses to ground truth by applying Procrustes analysis on camera pose locations. It is primarily used for pose quality assessment and as an initialization step for test-time optimization in neural radiance field (NeRF) reconstruction. Unlike [PCA Pose Alignment](7626-pca-pose-alignment.md), which aligns poses based on principal component analysis of their spatial distribution, this method explicitly minimizes the geometric error between corresponding camera centers under a similarity transform.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$ for computing means and cross products, where $N$ is the number of poses. The matrix inversion is $O(1)$ for a fixed $4 \times 4$ matrix. The final transformation is $O(N)$ for matrix multiplication.
-- **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ to store the input and output poses. Temporary storage for homogenized poses is also $O(N)$.
-- **Measured Performance:** The method is extremely fast, typically taking less than a millisecond for thousands of poses on a modern CPU.
-- **Strengths:** Simple to implement, numerically stable for typical SfM outputs, effectively centers the scene for NDC-based rendering.
-- **Limitations:** Assumes the average pose is a good representation of the scene center. May not work well for highly non-uniform camera distributions or scenes with large outliers.
-- **When to Use:** When preparing camera poses for volume rendering with NDC coordinates, especially for datasets like LLFF or Blender synthetic data where the camera distribution is roughly centered around the scene.
-- **When Not to Use:** When absolute scale is required (use [Sim(3) Alignment via Procrustes Analysis](8526-sim-3-alignment-via-procrustes-analysis.md)), or when the camera distribution is highly skewed or degenerate.
